#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538022} :activity/id #uuid "d03962de-53d8-58b6-98cc-85b49115e6d9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049315 :topic/id #uuid "eba98dcb-66f5-5f67-ad46-4c466f76f5ef" :topic/math-academy-id 4162} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d973586-88b0-574a-bc5a-aa7ae3cf60bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276312} {:answer/id #uuid "a0363b08-6d81-540c-bc68-0e8dd8bd679c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "92\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276921} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e87099-de5e-5c0f-a0a8-04a9e6edcfc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277200} {:answer/id #uuid "c30bc8bf-6789-54b4-bda7-d75d38ff9de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277211} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ce25d1-2666-5731-8c05-bbb57a907bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276312} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f5f1894-1715-5ace-b891-a801617f3574" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87215c50-3d7b-51ff-bbb4-0e6c4fa9c71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161233" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(x(x-y)+y(y+x)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =108\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "39301e03-b48e-51b6-bb45-a99701503350" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538033}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07586b26-eba9-5cc1-8cf4-551cd0a12810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275546} {:answer/id #uuid "1966fb47-2f70-52db-a0f2-04e992ce72b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275715} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0ec097-6f70-5bf8-a2f4-b9c4083e726f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277138} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b76320-8254-55c2-b4ea-f25d7daa4b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277504} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b3849e-050e-585f-a175-8267a557e9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277504} :answer-field/id #uuid "9529dcc8-3804-5b8d-be78-f7e1b203fd7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a533e788-011b-5fa3-9d77-cd8a43ced2a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161203" :question/problem "![](images/ce/cece4e226becb315d6d951a8ae782750da79209d9a67ec52a8cd4d134999e9e6.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/21/21e29d99d2d5330db2d946d981e4bd0f820ce26297bf95f962cb3000f0998136.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}(4-{r}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[2{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot (18-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-18\\pi +0 \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "52481592-db04-5beb-9a44-7dfde1d1ecfd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253bc184-740c-51c5-bffa-f791ca607ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275814} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9bb457-d829-5a5d-9a7b-819fd1e87d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276007} {:answer/id #uuid "90694ad8-9294-5db9-9c43-98c9fb606cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276785} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ffdca8-7e8f-5108-9f3c-ca4ffa31d862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277110} {:answer/id #uuid "cacec28a-9f0f-573f-b6ab-d2a206ad0d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276007} :answer-field/id #uuid "8284499e-ae11-52db-8d44-1ea843ffae18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c8f1bee6-220a-53d7-9c95-ba6fb2fa5c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-161241" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "87f36f5a-67a0-55ce-9ac0-719dbb07135b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c119139-8ea1-597d-a05f-d1c221c0f1b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi {e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275579} {:answer/id #uuid "244ef3b6-65d9-5030-b4e3-f8e8bed1a7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi (e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275802} {:answer/id #uuid "7235847c-f8a5-581f-910a-a0bc10eb269c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276511} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad89107-e00b-5cbf-bd1a-014745607d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276593} {:answer/id #uuid "c43f92bd-46e9-56dc-842f-256222ef8f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi ({e}^{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275802} :answer-field/id #uuid "c67151bb-afad-5510-9e88-7cde83548476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1daafe6b-326b-58b0-829c-25583c57b290" :question/math-academy-id "q-161215" :question/problem "![](images/53/534c80a8318ebfe21ba3f21f04ae145f999d6d83c34a402294c789bb2090e014.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/7f/7f44bc35687020594b0d26a2e25b9dbd2b8d75c964a90184626afad6afc1b1e8.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1)+0 \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "abb83d5b-b9f4-5ee1-8479-b852400c679a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1242a293-4ae5-5fa1-bfa5-f75baf035973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537261} {:answer/id #uuid "38c6cf47-a825-5070-89ae-ba194b490421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537345} {:answer/id #uuid "570a9294-3afd-5d27-ace4-dabede751046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537407} {:answer/id #uuid "753675fc-41c9-57ed-b147-8dbaf347a205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537462} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c9610c-4027-5e7b-a8f2-534eb909848c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537544} :answer-field/id #uuid "8118e8d0-ad36-5eed-a94c-c49d91624022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50078542-09f5-53cd-a63a-27e043674d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162562" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/dc/dc37a067eb8070ecbdfea53c1ede3e221e1873a192b50fcbf07234f33c183787.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=3$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12{y}^{2}{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4{y}^{3}{z}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}32{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[8{z}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4x{y}^{2}{z}^{3},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =8+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f03fe234-508d-516c-bf47-5e1dd4eafb5f"}] :activity/title "Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537217}]}
